 ## Overview
-Parallelism and Distribution refers to the family of techniques that map volume rendering computations across multiple processing units (CPUs, GPUs, or networked nodes) to handle datasets or frame rates that exceed single-device capabilities [G]. It solves the scalability bottleneck of rendering large, high-resolution, or time-varying volumes by partitioning the workload spatially, temporally, or algorithmically [G]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Tiled Parallel Compositing](1192-tiled-parallel-compositing.md) (which focuses on intra-node tile management) and [Compute Shader Kernel](1202-compute-shader-kernel.md) (which focuses on single-device kernel structure) by addressing inter-node communication, load balancing across heterogeneous clusters, and distributed memory management [G].
-
+Parallelism and Distribution refers to the family of techniques that map volume rendering computations across multiple processing units (CPUs, GPUs, or networked nodes) to handle datasets or frame rates that exceed single-device capabilities [G]. It solves the scalability bottleneck of rendering large, high-resolution, or time-varying volumes by partitioning the workload spatially, temporally, or algorithmically [G]. It builds on [Parallel and GPU primitives](771-parallel-and-gpu-primitives.md) and sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Tiled Parallel Compositing](1192-tiled-parallel-compositing.md) (which focuses on intra-node tile management) and [Compute Shader Kernel](1202-compute-shader-kernel.md) (which focuses on single-device kernel structure) by addressing inter-node communication, load balancing across heterogeneous clusters, and distributed memory management [G].
 ## Shared principles
-The core mechanism of this family is the mapping of computational tasks (pixels, rays, or data elements) to parallel execution units (threads, warps, or cores) [G]. Common principles include:
-1. **Thread Mapping:** Assigning one or more threads to process specific data elements (e.g., one thread per pixel or one thread per voxel sample) [G].
-2. **Memory Hierarchy Utilization:** Leveraging global memory for large datasets, shared/local memory for intermediate results (reductions), and registers for per-thread state [G].
-3. **Synchronization:** Managing concurrency conflicts using atomic operations, barriers, or spatial partitioning (tiles) to ensure data consistency without excessive serialization [G].
-4. **Data Parallelism:** Performing identical operations on different data elements simultaneously, often requiring coalesced memory access patterns to maximize bandwidth [G].
-
+This family inherits its core mechanism, thread mapping, memory hierarchy utilization, synchronization strategies, and data parallelism principles from [Parallel and GPU primitives](771-parallel-and-gpu-primitives.md).
